1
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:08,237
Turning from the Temple gate as soon as I had read the warning, I made the best of my way to Fleet

2
00:00:08,237 --> 00:00:14,237
Street, and there got a late hackney chariot and drove to the Hummums in Covent Garden. In

3
00:00:14,237 --> 00:00:18,678
those times a bed was always to be got there at any hour of the night, and the chamberlain,

4
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:23,641
letting me in at his ready wicket, lighted the candle next in order on his shelf,

5
00:00:23,641 --> 00:00:29,319
and showed me straight into the bedroom next in order on his list. It was a sort of vault on the

6
00:00:29,319 --> 00:00:35,597
ground floor at the back, with a despotic monster of a four-post bedstead in it, straddling over the

7
00:00:35,597 --> 00:00:41,358
whole place, putting one of his arbitrary legs into the fireplace and another into the doorway,

8
00:00:41,358 --> 00:00:47,319
and squeezing the wretched little washing-stand in quite a Divinely Righteous manner.

9
00:00:47,319 --> 00:00:52,796
As I had asked for a night-light, the chamberlain had brought me in, before he left me, the good old

10
00:00:52,796 --> 00:00:58,718
constitutional rushlight of those virtuous days—an object like the ghost of a walking-cane, which

11
00:00:58,718 --> 00:01:04,116
instantly broke its back if it were touched, which nothing could ever be lighted at, and which was

12
00:01:04,116 --> 00:01:09,917
placed in solitary confinement at the bottom of a high tin tower, perforated with round holes that

13
00:01:09,917 --> 00:01:17,398
made a staringly wide-awake pattern on the walls. When I had got into bed, and lay there footsore,

14
00:01:17,398 --> 00:01:23,838
weary, and wretched, I found that I could no more close my own eyes than I could close the eyes of

15
00:01:23,839 --> 00:01:31,398
this foolish Argus. And thus, in the gloom and death of the night, we stared at one another.

16
00:01:31,398 --> 00:01:39,957
What a doleful night! How anxious, how dismal, how long! There was an inhospitable smell in the room,

17
00:01:39,957 --> 00:01:46,160
of cold soot and hot dust; and, as I looked up into the corners of the tester over my head,

18
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:51,078
I thought what a number of blue-bottle flies from the butchers’, and earwigs from the market,

19
00:01:51,078 --> 00:01:57,398
and grubs from the country, must be holding on up there, lying by for next summer. This led me to

20
00:01:57,398 --> 00:02:02,358
speculate whether any of them ever tumbled down, and then I fancied that I felt light

21
00:02:02,358 --> 00:02:09,199
falls on my face,—a disagreeable turn of thought, suggesting other and more objectionable approaches

22
00:02:09,199 --> 00:02:15,237
up my back. When I had lain awake a little while, those extraordinary voices with which

23
00:02:15,237 --> 00:02:21,758
silence teems began to make themselves audible. The closet whispered, the fireplace sighed,

24
00:02:22,597 --> 00:02:27,038
the little washing-stand ticked, and one guitar-string played occasionally in the

25
00:02:27,038 --> 00:02:32,641
chest of drawers. At about the same time, the eyes on the wall acquired a new expression,

26
00:02:32,641 --> 00:02:37,917
and in every one of those staring rounds I saw written, DON’T GO HOME.

27
00:02:37,917 --> 00:02:44,519
Whatever night-fancies and night-noises crowded on me, they never warded off this DON’T GO HOME.

28
00:02:44,519 --> 00:02:51,398
It plaited itself into whatever I thought of, as a bodily pain would have done. Not long before,

29
00:02:51,398 --> 00:02:57,078
I had read in the newspapers, how a gentleman unknown had come to the Hummums in the night,

30
00:02:57,078 --> 00:03:02,199
and had gone to bed, and had destroyed himself, and had been found in the morning weltering

31
00:03:02,199 --> 00:03:09,436
in blood. It came into my head that he must have occupied this very vault of mine, and I got out of

32
00:03:09,438 --> 00:03:14,879
bed to assure myself that there were no red marks about; then opened the door to look out into the

33
00:03:14,879 --> 00:03:20,199
passages, and cheer myself with the companionship of a distant light, near which I knew the

34
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:27,000
chamberlain to be dozing. But all this time, why I was not to go home, and what had happened at home,

35
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,438
and when I should go home, and whether Provis was safe at home, were questions occupying my mind so

36
00:03:33,438 --> 00:03:39,358
busily, that one might have supposed there could be no more room in it for any other theme. Even

37
00:03:39,358 --> 00:03:44,756
when I thought of Estella, and how we had parted that day forever, and when I recalled all the

38
00:03:44,758 --> 00:03:50,796
circumstances of our parting, and all her looks and tones, and the action of her fingers while she

39
00:03:50,796 --> 00:03:58,116
knitted,—even then I was pursuing, here and there and everywhere, the caution, Don’t go home. When

40
00:03:58,116 --> 00:04:04,277
at last I dozed, in sheer exhaustion of mind and body, it became a vast shadowy verb which I had

41
00:04:04,277 --> 00:04:12,639
to conjugate. Imperative mood, present tense: Do not thou go home, let him not go home, let us not

42
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:20,277
go home, do not ye or you go home, let not them go home. Then potentially: I may not and I cannot go

43
00:04:20,277 --> 00:04:26,436
home; and I might not, could not, would not, and should not go home; until I felt that I was going

44
00:04:26,438 --> 00:04:33,116
distracted, and rolled over on the pillow, and looked at the staring rounds upon the wall again.

45
00:04:33,116 --> 00:04:38,000
I had left directions that I was to be called at seven; for it was plain that I must see Wemmick

46
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,078
before seeing any one else, and equally plain that this was a case in which his Walworth sentiments

47
00:04:44,078 --> 00:04:51,277
only could be taken. It was a relief to get out of the room where the night had been so miserable,

48
00:04:51,277 --> 00:04:56,436
and I needed no second knocking at the door to startle me from my uneasy bed.

49
00:04:56,438 --> 00:05:01,641
The Castle battlements arose upon my view at eight o’clock. The little servant happening to be

50
00:05:01,641 --> 00:05:07,597
entering the fortress with two hot rolls, I passed through the postern and crossed the drawbridge in

51
00:05:07,597 --> 00:05:13,718
her company, and so came without announcement into the presence of Wemmick as he was making

52
00:05:13,718 --> 00:05:21,559
tea for himself and the Aged. An open door afforded a perspective view of the Aged in bed.

53
00:05:22,078 --> 00:05:27,758
“Halloa, Mr. Pip!” said Wemmick. “You did come home, then?”

54
00:05:27,758 --> 00:05:31,480
“Yes,” I returned; “but I didn’t go home.”

55
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:35,718
“That’s all right,” said he, rubbing his hands. “I left a note for you

56
00:05:35,718 --> 00:05:40,078
at each of the Temple gates, on the chance. Which gate did you come to?”

57
00:05:40,078 --> 00:05:42,000
I told him.

58
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,237
“I’ll go round to the others in the course of the day and destroy the notes,” said Wemmick;

59
00:05:47,237 --> 00:05:50,758
“it’s a good rule never to leave documentary evidence if you can help it,

60
00:05:50,758 --> 00:05:54,519
because you don’t know when it may be put in. I’m going to take a liberty

61
00:05:54,519 --> 00:05:59,116
with you. Would you mind toasting this sausage for the Aged P.?”

62
00:05:59,116 --> 00:06:02,038
I said I should be delighted to do it.

63
00:06:02,038 --> 00:06:06,319
“Then you can go about your work, Mary Anne,” said Wemmick to the little servant;

64
00:06:06,319 --> 00:06:12,519
“which leaves us to ourselves, don’t you see, Mr. Pip?” he added, winking, as she disappeared.

65
00:06:12,519 --> 00:06:17,438
I thanked him for his friendship and caution, and our discourse proceeded in a low tone,

66
00:06:17,438 --> 00:06:23,237
while I toasted the Aged’s sausage and he buttered the crumb of the Aged’s roll.

67
00:06:23,237 --> 00:06:29,796
“Now, Mr. Pip, you know,” said Wemmick, “you and I understand one another. We are in our

68
00:06:29,796 --> 00:06:34,680
private and personal capacities, and we have been engaged in a confidential transaction

69
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:41,319
before to-day. Official sentiments are one thing. We are extra official.”

70
00:06:41,319 --> 00:06:44,557
I cordially assented. I was so very nervous,

71
00:06:44,559 --> 00:06:51,277
that I had already lighted the Aged’s sausage like a torch, and been obliged to blow it out.

72
00:06:51,277 --> 00:06:56,756
“I accidentally heard, yesterday morning,” said Wemmick, “being in a certain place where I once

73
00:06:56,758 --> 00:07:03,680
took you,—even between you and me, it’s as well not to mention names when avoidable—”

74
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:07,718
“Much better not,” said I. “I understand you.”

75
00:07:07,718 --> 00:07:11,038
“I heard there by chance, yesterday morning,” said Wemmick,

76
00:07:11,038 --> 00:07:15,398
“that a certain person not altogether of uncolonial pursuits, and not unpossessed

77
00:07:15,398 --> 00:07:20,877
of portable property,—I don’t know who it may really be,—we won’t name this person—”

78
00:07:20,879 --> 00:07:22,718
“Not necessary,” said I.

79
00:07:22,718 --> 00:07:27,480
“—Had made some little stir in a certain part of the world where a good many people go, not always

80
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:33,796
in gratification of their own inclinations, and not quite irrespective of the government expense—”

81
00:07:33,796 --> 00:07:37,839
In watching his face, I made quite a firework of the Aged’s sausage,

82
00:07:37,839 --> 00:07:43,480
and greatly discomposed both my own attention and Wemmick’s; for which I apologised.

83
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:50,078
“—By disappearing from such place, and being no more heard of thereabouts. From which,” said

84
00:07:50,078 --> 00:07:55,839
Wemmick, “conjectures had been raised and theories formed. I also heard that you at

85
00:07:55,839 --> 00:08:01,358
your chambers in Garden Court, Temple, had been watched, and might be watched again.”

86
00:08:01,358 --> 00:08:03,957
“By whom?” said I.

87
00:08:03,957 --> 00:08:08,358
“I wouldn’t go into that,” said Wemmick, evasively, “it might clash with official

88
00:08:08,358 --> 00:08:13,000
responsibilities. I heard it, as I have in my time heard other curious

89
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:20,319
things in the same place. I don’t tell it you on information received. I heard it.”

90
00:08:20,319 --> 00:08:25,116
He took the toasting-fork and sausage from me as he spoke, and set forth the Aged’s

91
00:08:25,116 --> 00:08:31,680
breakfast neatly on a little tray. Previous to placing it before him, he went into the Aged’s

92
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:37,199
room with a clean white cloth, and tied the same under the old gentleman’s chin,

93
00:08:37,199 --> 00:08:43,839
and propped him up, and put his nightcap on one side, and gave him quite a rakish air.

94
00:08:43,839 --> 00:08:48,879
Then he placed his breakfast before him with great care, and said, “All right, ain’t you,

95
00:08:48,879 --> 00:08:57,879
Aged P.?” To which the cheerful Aged replied, “All right, John, my boy, all right!” As there

96
00:08:57,879 --> 00:09:02,796
seemed to be a tacit understanding that the Aged was not in a presentable state, and was therefore

97
00:09:02,796 --> 00:09:09,277
to be considered invisible, I made a pretence of being in complete ignorance of these proceedings.

98
00:09:09,277 --> 00:09:14,237
“This watching of me at my chambers (which I have once had reason to suspect),” I said to

99
00:09:14,237 --> 00:09:21,319
Wemmick when he came back, “is inseparable from the person to whom you have adverted; is it?”

100
00:09:21,879 --> 00:09:26,680
Wemmick looked very serious. “I couldn’t undertake to say that,

101
00:09:26,680 --> 00:09:30,237
of my own knowledge. I mean, I couldn’t undertake to say it was

102
00:09:30,237 --> 00:09:36,917
at first. But it either is, or it will be, or it’s in great danger of being.”

103
00:09:36,917 --> 00:09:42,398
As I saw that he was restrained by fealty to Little Britain from saying as much as he could,

104
00:09:42,398 --> 00:09:48,319
and as I knew with thankfulness to him how far out of his way he went to say what he did,

105
00:09:48,319 --> 00:09:53,480
I could not press him. But I told him, after a little meditation over the fire,

106
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:58,319
that I would like to ask him a question, subject to his answering or not answering,

107
00:09:58,319 --> 00:10:05,116
as he deemed right, and sure that his course would be right. He paused in his breakfast,

108
00:10:05,116 --> 00:10:09,758
and crossing his arms, and pinching his shirt-sleeves (his notion of in-door

109
00:10:09,758 --> 00:10:16,237
comfort was to sit without any coat), he nodded to me once, to put my question.

110
00:10:16,237 --> 00:10:21,237
“You have heard of a man of bad character, whose true name is Compeyson?”

111
00:10:22,038 --> 00:10:24,358
He answered with one other nod.

112
00:10:24,358 --> 00:10:25,796
“Is he living?”

113
00:10:25,796 --> 00:10:27,277
One other nod.

114
00:10:27,277 --> 00:10:29,519
“Is he in London?”

115
00:10:29,519 --> 00:10:33,398
He gave me one other nod, compressed the post-office exceedingly,

116
00:10:33,398 --> 00:10:37,718
gave me one last nod, and went on with his breakfast.

117
00:10:37,718 --> 00:10:44,237
“Now,” said Wemmick, “questioning being over,” which he emphasised and repeated for my guidance,

118
00:10:44,237 --> 00:10:50,480
“I come to what I did, after hearing what I heard. I went to Garden Court to find you; not finding

119
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:58,796
you, I went to Clarriker’s to find Mr. Herbert.” “And him you found?” said I, with great anxiety.

120
00:10:58,796 --> 00:11:04,641
“And him I found. Without mentioning any names or going into any details,

121
00:11:04,641 --> 00:11:08,398
I gave him to understand that if he was aware of anybody—Tom, Jack,

122
00:11:08,398 --> 00:11:12,756
or Richard—being about the chambers, or about the immediate neighbourhood,

123
00:11:12,758 --> 00:11:18,438
he had better get Tom, Jack, or Richard out of the way while you were out of the way.”

124
00:11:18,438 --> 00:11:21,319
“He would be greatly puzzled what to do?”

125
00:11:21,319 --> 00:11:25,879
“He was puzzled what to do; not the less, because I gave him my opinion that it was

126
00:11:25,879 --> 00:11:32,199
not safe to try to get Tom, Jack, or Richard too far out of the way at present. Mr. Pip,

127
00:11:32,199 --> 00:11:36,358
I’ll tell you something. Under existing circumstances, there is no place like

128
00:11:36,358 --> 00:11:41,519
a great city when you are once in it. Don’t break cover too soon. Lie close.

129
00:11:41,519 --> 00:11:46,237
Wait till things slacken, before you try the open, even for foreign air.”

130
00:11:46,237 --> 00:11:51,160
I thanked him for his valuable advice, and asked him what Herbert had done?

131
00:11:51,160 --> 00:11:55,277
“Mr. Herbert,” said Wemmick, “after being all of a heap for half an hour,

132
00:11:55,277 --> 00:11:58,957
struck out a plan. He mentioned to me as a secret,

133
00:11:58,957 --> 00:12:05,277
that he is courting a young lady who has, as no doubt you are aware, a bedridden Pa. Which Pa,

134
00:12:05,277 --> 00:12:09,756
having been in the Purser line of life, lies a-bed in a bow-window where he can

135
00:12:09,758 --> 00:12:15,480
see the ships sail up and down the river. You are acquainted with the young lady, most probably?”

136
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:18,519
“Not personally,” said I.

137
00:12:18,519 --> 00:12:25,160
The truth was, that she had objected to me as an expensive companion who did Herbert no good, and

138
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:30,718
that, when Herbert had first proposed to present me to her, she had received the proposal with

139
00:12:30,718 --> 00:12:36,319
such very moderate warmth, that Herbert had felt himself obliged to confide the state of the case

140
00:12:36,319 --> 00:12:42,678
to me, with a view to the lapse of a little time before I made her acquaintance. When I had begun

141
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:48,480
to advance Herbert’s prospects by stealth, I had been able to bear this with cheerful philosophy:

142
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:53,639
he and his affianced, for their part, had naturally not been very anxious to introduce

143
00:12:53,641 --> 00:12:59,917
a third person into their interviews; and thus, although I was assured that I had risen in Clara’s

144
00:12:59,917 --> 00:13:05,237
esteem, and although the young lady and I had long regularly interchanged messages and remembrances

145
00:13:05,237 --> 00:13:12,879
by Herbert, I had never seen her. However, I did not trouble Wemmick with these particulars.

146
00:13:12,879 --> 00:13:17,038
“The house with the bow-window,” said Wemmick, “being by the river-side,

147
00:13:17,038 --> 00:13:21,038
down the Pool there between Limehouse and Greenwich, and being kept, it seems,

148
00:13:21,559 --> 00:13:27,480
by a very respectable widow who has a furnished upper floor to let, Mr. Herbert put it to me,

149
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:33,480
what did I think of that as a temporary tenement for Tom, Jack, or Richard? Now,

150
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:41,116
I thought very well of it, for three reasons I’ll give you. That is to say: Firstly. It’s

151
00:13:41,116 --> 00:13:46,038
altogether out of all your beats, and is well away from the usual heap of streets great and

152
00:13:46,038 --> 00:13:53,597
small. Secondly. Without going near it yourself, you could always hear of the safety of Tom, Jack,

153
00:13:53,597 --> 00:14:00,358
or Richard, through Mr. Herbert. Thirdly. After a while and when it might be prudent, if you should

154
00:14:00,358 --> 00:14:06,398
want to slip Tom, Jack, or Richard on board a foreign packet-boat, there he is—ready.”

155
00:14:06,398 --> 00:14:08,796
Much comforted by these considerations,

156
00:14:08,796 --> 00:14:13,358
I thanked Wemmick again and again, and begged him to proceed.

157
00:14:13,358 --> 00:14:18,639
“Well, sir! Mr. Herbert threw himself into the business with a will, and by nine o’clock last

158
00:14:18,641 --> 00:14:23,796
night he housed Tom, Jack, or Richard,—whichever it may be,—you and I don’t want to know,—quite

159
00:14:23,796 --> 00:14:28,718
successfully. At the old lodgings it was understood that he was summoned to Dover,

160
00:14:28,718 --> 00:14:35,758
and, in fact, he was taken down the Dover road and cornered out of it. Now, another great advantage

161
00:14:35,758 --> 00:14:42,319
of all this is, that it was done without you, and when, if any one was concerning himself about your

162
00:14:42,319 --> 00:14:48,480
movements, you must be known to be ever so many miles off and quite otherwise engaged.

163
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:54,319
This diverts suspicion and confuses it; and for the same reason I recommended that, even if you

164
00:14:54,319 --> 00:15:01,358
came back last night, you should not go home. It brings in more confusion, and you want confusion.”

165
00:15:01,358 --> 00:15:07,436
Wemmick, having finished his breakfast, here looked at his watch, and began to get his coat on.

166
00:15:07,438 --> 00:15:11,718
“And now, Mr. Pip,” said he, with his hands still in the sleeves,

167
00:15:11,718 --> 00:15:17,917
“I have probably done the most I can do; but if I can ever do more,—from a Walworth point of view,

168
00:15:17,917 --> 00:15:24,879
and in a strictly private and personal capacity,—I shall be glad to do it. Here’s the address. There

169
00:15:24,879 --> 00:15:29,758
can be no harm in your going here to-night, and seeing for yourself that all is well with Tom,

170
00:15:29,758 --> 00:15:34,160
Jack, or Richard, before you go home,—which is another reason for your not going home

171
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:39,480
last night. But, after you have gone home, don’t go back here. You are very welcome,

172
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:45,078
I am sure, Mr. Pip”; his hands were now out of his sleeves, and I was shaking them;

173
00:15:45,078 --> 00:15:50,917
“and let me finally impress one important point upon you.” He laid his hands upon my shoulders,

174
00:15:50,917 --> 00:15:57,358
and added in a solemn whisper: “Avail yourself of this evening to lay hold of his portable property.

175
00:15:57,358 --> 00:16:03,078
You don’t know what may happen to him. Don’t let anything happen to the portable property.”

176
00:16:03,078 --> 00:16:09,917
Quite despairing of making my mind clear to Wemmick on this point, I forbore to try.

177
00:16:09,917 --> 00:16:14,917
“Time’s up,” said Wemmick, “and I must be off. If you had nothing more pressing to do than to keep

178
00:16:14,917 --> 00:16:21,078
here till dark, that’s what I should advise. You look very much worried, and it would do you good

179
00:16:21,078 --> 00:16:25,480
to have a perfectly quiet day with the Aged,—he’ll be up presently,—and a little

180
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:30,038
bit of—you remember the pig?” “Of course,” said I.

181
00:16:30,038 --> 00:16:34,680
“Well; and a little bit of him. That sausage you toasted was his,

182
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:38,038
and he was in all respects a first-rater. Do try him,

183
00:16:38,038 --> 00:16:45,000
if it is only for old acquaintance sake. Good-bye, Aged Parent!” in a cheery shout.

184
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:49,796
“All right, John; all right, my boy!” piped the old man from within.

185
00:16:49,796 --> 00:16:54,559
I soon fell asleep before Wemmick’s fire, and the Aged and I enjoyed one another’s society

186
00:16:54,559 --> 00:16:59,796
by falling asleep before it more or less all day. We had loin of pork for dinner,

187
00:16:59,796 --> 00:17:05,519
and greens grown on the estate; and I nodded at the Aged with a good intention whenever I failed

188
00:17:05,519 --> 00:17:12,438
to do it drowsily. When it was quite dark, I left the Aged preparing the fire for toast;

189
00:17:12,438 --> 00:17:17,000
and I inferred from the number of teacups, as well as from his glances at the two little doors in the

190
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,991
wall, that Miss Skiffins was expected. Chapter 46.
